ad_impact_cc:
The PNACC vulnerability analysis revealed that the country's most vulnerable areas are "Sahel Sudanese EEZ and the coastal monomodal rainfall AEZ;" and the most vulnerable sectors are "(i) agriculture, and (ii) water, sanitation and health;" (1) 

"-Agriculture: Vulnerability will be high to very high overall in zones 1, 2 and 4, high to medium in the rest of the country (droughts, rise in temperatures);

-Livestock: Vulnerability will be high to very high overall in zones 1, 2 and 3, high to medium in the rest of the country (droughts, rise in temperatures);

- Fishing and aquaculture: Vulnerability will be generally high to medium throughout the country, especially in zones 5, 1, and 4, high to medium in the rest of the country (droughts, rise in temperatures);

- Forestry, forestry and fauna: Vulnerability will be high to very high overall in zones 4 and 5, high to medium in the rest of the country but with strong trends in woodlands (droughts, extreme events);

- Water, sanitation and health: Vulnerability will be high to very high overall in zones 4 and 5, high to medium in the rest of the country but with strong trends in woodlands (drought, floods and land movement);

- Energy, mines and industries: Vulnerability will be high to very high overall in zones 5 and 2, high to medium in the rest of the country (rainfall, drought, rise in sea level);

- Urban development and public works: Overall vulnerability will be high to very high in zones 5 and 4, high to medium in the rest of the country (floods, rise in sea level);

- Tourism: Vulnerability will be medium to low overall in the country, except in the mountain ranges and zone 1 (Drought)." (1)

ad_vision:
“As a result, national adaptation priorities have been defined for 235, broken down into multiple strategies. Concerning adaptation, this will entail:  

-Reducing vulnerability to drought;  

-Protecting against rising sea levels;  

-Improving access to water;  

-Protecting biodiversity;  

-Reinforcing the resilience of rural populations.” (p. 9)

ad_plan_adp:
"Algeria aims to develop a national plan of adaptation to the impacts of climate change in the context of the finalization of its contribution, and in order to promote a more climate change resilient economy. Priority will be given to the protection of the population and the preservation of natural resources and key infrastructure against the risks of extreme events.
The objective of this national plan is:
- To reinforce the ecosystems resilience (flooding and drought) in order to curtail the risks of natural disasters related to climate change;
- To fight against erosion and rehabilitate its degraded lands as part of the
efforts to combat desertification ;
- To integrate the impacts of climate change into sectorial strategies, in particular for agriculture, water management, public health and transport;
- To integrate the impacts of climate change on political stability and national
security." (8)

"For that purpose, Algeria is committed to finalize and adopt its National Climate Plan before the 21st Conference of the Parties. " (1)

ad_nonfin:
“If the INDC commitments are to be fulfilled, four urgent cross-sector measures must be implemented with support from the international community: 

- Meet the significant needs for robust, accessible data, cross-sector medium-term strategic planning documents and monitoring of natural resources (gathering data on water and forest resources, together with meteorological data); these needs can be partially covered through bilateral agreements with some regional-scale organizations;  

- Promote the inclusion of climate change issues, especially regarding adaptation, in planning and budgeting at local, sectoral and national level;  

- Take account of gender issues in all development programmes and projects;  

- Make information on environmental law and the causes and impacts of climate change widely available and accessible so as to raise public awareness and educate the Guinean population as a whole.” (p. 13)

